This version of Hercules is a modification by Paul
Edwards to standard Hercules 3.07 from:

http://www.hercules-390.org

to support the S/380 architecture.

This version of Hercules/380 should have pretty much
all z/Arch instructions enabled. But note that you
can no longer use a 1052 terminal, you need to use
3215 instead. That's because 1052 has been repurposed
to produce an EBCDIC ANSI terminal for use with
micro-emacs under PDOS.

If you wish to recreate the source code (Unix users
must do this), you will need to go there and download 
the Hercules 3.07 source code and then apply the patch 
provided here (hercules380*.txt) using the Unix "patch" 
utility (or equivalent). E.g. if you are in the same
directory as this readme file, you would use a command
something like this:

patch -p 1 <hercules380...txt

Also see the COPYRIGHT file and go to that HTML page
to read the license agreement.


Tip for people needing source.  The additional source
you need can be obtained from here:
http://www.pcre.org/
http://www.zlib.net/
http://www.bzip.org/

Note that as of Hercules/380 3.06 v 6.0, choosing S/370
will have no effect - it will be S/380. This is until
Fish's Hercgui is updated to recognize S/380. If you
require a plain S/370, just run normal Hercules. Obviously
you will lose the various enhancements such as extra
TDF tape formats if you do that.

Also check this if you are recompiling from source on
Windows and don't have access to VC2005:

http://www.softdevlabs.com/Hercules/hercules-msvc-build.html

Although what I am actually using is:

Download VC2005 from:

http://download.microsoft.com/download/8/3/a/83aad8f9-38ba-4503-b3cd-ba28c360c27b/ENU/vcsetup.exe

Download SDK from here:

http://www.microsoft.com/en-gb/download/confirmation.aspx?id=3138

Run this batch file:

if %ZLIB_DIR%. == . goto needset
goto bypass
:needset
rem call setenv /XP32 /RETAIL
rem call setenv /release /x86 /xp
call setenv /release /x86 /xp
path %path%;C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft Visual Studio 8\VC\bin;c:\winpath;C:\Windows\System32
call vcvars32
set ZLIB_DIR=c:\zlib
set PCRE_DIR=c:\pcre
set BZIP2_DIR=c:\bzip2
rem set CFG=RETAIL
rem patch -p 1 <../hercules380-3_05-1_0.txt
:bypass
rem nmake -f makefile.msvc clean
rem nmake -f makefile.msvc
nmake -f makefile-dllmod.msvc clean
nmake -f makefile-dllmod.msvc


Or at least, I was. Now I believe I am using VC2008,
which I think is what allows 64-bit building, so I
now have 32-bit building:

if %ZLIB_DIR%. == . goto needset
goto bypass
:needset
rem call setenv /XP32 /RETAIL
call setenv /release /x86 /xp
rem call setenv /release /x64 /xp
path %path%;C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft Visual Studio 9.0\VC\bin;c:\winpath;C:\Windows\System32
call vcvars32
set ZLIB_DIR=c:\zlib
set PCRE_DIR=c:\pcre
set BZIP2_DIR=c:\bzip2
rem set CFG=RETAIL
rem patch -p 1 <../hercules380-3_05-1_0.txt
:bypass
rem nmake -f makefile.msvc clean
rem nmake -f makefile.msvc
nmake -f makefile-dllmod.msvc clean
nmake -f makefile-dllmod.msvc

and 64-bit building:

if %ZLIB_DIR%. == . goto needset
goto bypass
:needset
rem call setenv /XP32 /RETAIL
rem call setenv /release /x86 /xp
call setenv /release /x64 /xp
path %path%;C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft Visual Studio 9.0\VC\bin;c:\winpath;C:\Windows\System32
call vcvars64
set ZLIB_DIR=c:\zlib
set PCRE_DIR=c:\pcre
set BZIP2_DIR=c:\bzip2
rem set CFG=RETAIL
rem patch -p 1 <../hercules380-3_05-1_0.txt
:bypass
rem nmake -f makefile.msvc clean
rem nmake -f makefile.msvc
nmake -f makefile-dllmod.msvc clean
nmake -f makefile-dllmod.msvc


Every time I upgrade laptop I have a nightmare of
how to install the Microsoft C compiler that works.
